doc:sch-2-sec-54-3-ss-3 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "54(3) Guarantee as to acceptable quality" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(3) The matters for the purposes of subsection (2) are:  (a) the nature of the goods; and  (b) the price of the goods (if relevant); and  (c) any statements made about the goods on any packaging or label on the goods; and  (d) any representation made about the goods by the supplier or manufacturer of the goods; and  (e) any other relevant circumstances relating to the supply of the goods. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  Schedule 1  The Australian Consumer Law" ;
    cof:pageNumber "85"^^xsd:integer .

concept:act-sch-2-sec-80-2 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "80 Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2)"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-80-2 ;
    values:originalText "(4) If:  (a) goods supplied to a consumer are not of acceptable quality; and  (b) the only reason or reasons why they are not of acceptable quality were specifically drawn to the consumer’s attention before the consumer agreed to the supply; the goods are taken to be of acceptable quality.  (5) If:  (a) goods are displayed for sale or hire; and  (b) the goods would not be of acceptable quality if they were supplied to a consumer; the reason or reasons why they are not of acceptable quality are taken, for the purposes of subsection (4), to have been specifically drawn to a consumer’s attention if those reasons were disclosed on a written notice that was displayed with the goods and that was transparent.  (6) Goods do not fail to be of acceptable quality if:  (a) the consumer to whom they are supplied causes them to become of unacceptable quality, or fails to take reasonable steps to prevent them from becoming of unacceptable quality; and  (b) they are damaged by abnormal use.  (7) Goods do not fail to be of acceptable quality if:  (a) the consumer acquiring the goods examines them before the consumer agrees to the supply of the goods; and  (b) the examination ought reasonably to have revealed that the goods were not of acceptable quality. 55  Guarantee as to fitness for any disclosed purpose etc.  (1) If:  (a) a person (the supplier) supplies, in trade or commerce, goods to a consumer; and  (b) the supply does not occur by way of sale by auction; there is a guarantee that the goods are reasonably fit for any disclosed purpose, and for any purpose for which the supplier represents that they are reasonably fit. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  The Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 1  Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2) 2010  (2) A disclosed purpose is a particular purpose (whether or not that purpose is a purpose for which the goods are commonly supplied) for which the goods are being acquired by the consumer and that:  (a) the consumer makes known, expressly or by implication, to:  (i) the supplier; or  (ii) a person by whom any prior negotiations or arrangements in relation to the acquisition of the goods were conducted or made; or  (b) the consumer makes known to the manufacturer of the goods either directly or through the supplier or the person referred to in paragraph (a)(ii).  (3) This section does not apply if the circumstances show that the consumer did not rely on, or that it was unreasonable for the consumer to rely on, the skill or judgment of the supplier, the person referred to in subsection (2)(a)(ii) or the manufacturer, as the case may be." ;
    skos:definition "(4) If:  (a) goods supplied to a consumer are not of acceptable quality; and  (b) the only reason or reasons why they are not of acceptable quality were specifically drawn to the consumer’s attention before the consumer agreed to the supply; the goods are taken to be of acceptable quality.  (5) If:  (a) goods are displayed for sale or hire; and  (b) the goods would not be of acceptable quality if they were supplied to a consumer; the reason or reasons why they are not of acceptable quality are taken, for the purposes of subsection (4), to have been specifically drawn to a consumer’s attention if those reasons were disclosed on a written notice that was displayed with the goods and that was transparent.  (6) Goods do not fail to be of acceptable quality if:  (a) the consumer to whom they are supplied causes them to become of unacceptable quality, or fails to take reasonable steps to prevent them from becoming of unacceptable quality; and  (b) they are damaged by abnormal use.  (7) Goods do not fail to be of acceptable quality if:  (a) the consumer acquiring the goods examines them before the consumer agrees to the supply of the goods; and  (b) the examination ought reasonably to have revealed that the goods were not of acceptable quality. 55  Guarantee as to fitness for any disclosed purpose etc.  (1) If:  (a) a person (the supplier) supplies, in trade or commerce, goods to a consumer; and  (b) the supply does not occur by way of sale by auction; there is a guarantee that the goods are reasonably fit for any disclosed purpose, and for any purpose for which the supplier represents that they are reasonably fit. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  The Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 1  Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2) 2010  (2) A disclosed purpose is a particular purpose (whether or not that purpose is a purpose for which the goods are commonly supplied) for which the goods are being acquired by the consumer and that:  (a) the consumer"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "5b2aa35e1044c79509735e54c6ec9a28dbf2364f36c39774682fb3d1d0dba40f" ;
    cof:pageNumber "86"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-80-2-ss-4 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-80-2-ss-5 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-80-2-ss-6 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-80-2-ss-7 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-80-2-ss-1 ;
