concept:act-sch-2-sec-86-2 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "86 Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2)"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-86-2 ;
    values:originalText "that it is not fair or reasonable for the person who supplied the goods or services to rely on that term of the contract.  (4) In determining for the purposes of subsection (3) whether or not reliance on a term of a contract is fair or reasonable, a court is to have regard to all the circumstances of the case, and in particular to the following matters:  (a) the strength of the bargaining positions of the person who supplied the goods or services and the person to whom the goods or services were supplied (the buyer) relative to each other, taking into account, among other things, the availability of equivalent goods or services and suitable alternative sources of supply;  (b) whether the buyer received an inducement to agree to the term or, in agreeing to the term, had an opportunity of acquiring the goods or services or equivalent goods or services from any source of supply under a contract that did not include that term;  (c) whether the buyer knew or ought reasonably to have known of the existence and extent of the term (having regard, among other things, to any custom of the trade and any previous course of dealing between the parties);  (d) in the case of the supply of goods, whether the goods were manufactured, processed or adapted to the special order of the buyer. Subdivision D—Miscellaneous" ;
    skos:definition "that it is not fair or reasonable for the person who supplied the goods or services to rely on that term of the contract.  (4) In determining for the purposes of subsection (3) whether or not reliance on a term of a contract is fair or reasonable, a court is to have regard to all the circumstances of the case, and in particular to the following matters:  (a) the strength of the bargaining positions of the person who supplied the goods or services and the person to whom the goods or services were supplied (the buyer) relative to each other, taking into account, among other things, the availability of equivalent goods or services and suitable alternative sources of supply;  (b) whether the buyer received an inducement to agree to the term or, in agreeing to the term, had an opportunity of acquiring the goods or services or equivalent goods or services from any source of supply under a contract that did not include that term;  (c) whether the buyer knew or ought reasonably to have known of the existence and extent of the term (having regard, among other things, to any custom of the trade and any previous course of dealing between the parties);  (d) in the case of the supply of goods, whether the goods were manufactured, processed or adapted to the special order of the buyer. Subdivision D—Miscellaneous"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "cf14a3b39d008968b885cb0c865a186687d997c55e6b4074158aed3db66ec8e5" ;
    cof:pageNumber "92"^^xsd:integer ;
    skos:note "Court must consider circumstances when determining if reliance on contract term is fair or reasonable." ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <act.pdf> ;
    dc:source "act.pdf, p.92" ;
    cml:asserts concept:act-sch-2-sec-86-2-norm .
