concept:act-sch-2-sec-101-2 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "101 Consumer may request an itemised bill"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-101-2 ;
    values:originalText "(1) If a person (the supplier), in trade or commerce, supplies services to a consumer, the consumer may request that the supplier give the consumer an itemised bill that:  (a) specifies how the price of the services was calculated; and  (b) includes, if applicable, the number of hours of labour that related to the supply of the services and the hourly rate for that labour; and  (c) includes, if applicable, a list of the materials used to supply the services and the amount charged for those materials.  (2) The request under subsection (1) must be made within 30 days after:  (a) the services are supplied; or  (b) the consumer receives a bill or account from the supplier for the supply of the services; whichever occurs later.  (3) The supplier must give the consumer the itemised bill within 7 days after the request is made. Note: A pecuniary penalty may be imposed for a contravention of this subsection.  (4) The supplier must not charge the consumer for the itemised bill. Note: A pecuniary penalty may be imposed for a contravention of this subsection.  (5) The supplier must ensure that the itemised bill is transparent. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  The Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 1  Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2) 2010" ;
    skos:definition "(1) If a person (the supplier), in trade or commerce, supplies services to a consumer, the consumer may request that the supplier give the consumer an itemised bill that:  (a) specifies how the price of the services was calculated; and  (b) includes, if applicable, the number of hours of labour that related to the supply of the services and the hourly rate for that labour; and  (c) includes, if applicable, a list of the materials used to supply the services and the amount charged for those materials.  (2) The request under subsection (1) must be made within 30 days after:  (a) the services are supplied; or  (b) the consumer receives a bill or account from the supplier for the supply of the services; whichever occurs later.  (3) The supplier must give the consumer the itemised bill within 7 days after the request is made. Note: A pecuniary penalty may be imposed for a contravention of this subsection.  (4) The supplier must not charge the consumer for the itemised bill. Note: A pecuniary penalty may be imposed for a contravention of this subsection.  (5) The supplier must ensure that the itemised bill is transparent. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  The Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 1  Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2) 2010"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "865033ca11e9a1cf9acc89ba376ecaa5974d9f7dffe9821f2bb2e3b425e5766e" ;
    cof:pageNumber "114"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-101-2-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-101-2-ss-2 ;
