concept:act-sch-2-sec-312 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "312 Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2)"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-312 ;
    values:originalText "Attachment  (9) The judge must attach to the documents provided under subsection (8) the warrant signed by the judge. Authority of warrant  (10) A form of warrant duly completed under subsection (7) is authority for the same powers as are authorised by the warrant signed by the judge.  (11) If:  (a) it is material, in any proceedings, for a court to be satisfied that an exercise of a power was authorised by this section; and  (b) the warrant signed by the judge authorising the exercise of the power is not produced in evidence; the court must assume, unless the contrary is proved, that the exercise of the power was not authorised by such a warrant. 136A  Offence relating to warrants by telephone, fax etc.  An inspector commits an offence if the inspector:  (a) states in a document that purports to be a form of warrant under section 136 the name of a judge unless that judge signed the warrant; or  (b) states on a form of warrant under that section a matter that, to the inspector’s knowledge, departs in a material particular from the terms of the warrant signed by the judge under that section; or  (c) purports to execute, or presents to another person, a document that purports to be a form of warrant under that section that the inspector knows:  (i) has not been approved by a judge under that section; or  (ii) departs in a material particular from the terms of a warrant signed by a judge under that section; or  (d) gives to a judge a form of warrant under that section that is not the form of warrant that the inspector purported to execute. Penalty: 120 penalty units or imprisonment for 2 years, or both. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  Application of the Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 2  Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2) 2010 Subdivision G—Miscellaneous" ;
    skos:definition "Attachment  (9) The judge must attach to the documents provided under subsection (8) the warrant signed by the judge. Authority of warrant  (10) A form of warrant duly completed under subsection (7) is authority for the same powers as are authorised by the warrant signed by the judge.  (11) If:  (a) it is material, in any proceedings, for a court to be satisfied that an exercise of a power was authorised by this section; and  (b) the warrant signed by the judge authorising the exercise of the power is not produced in evidence; the court must assume, unless the contrary is proved, that the exercise of the power was not authorised by such a warrant. 136A  Offence relating to warrants by telephone, fax etc.  An inspector commits an offence if the inspector:  (a) states in a document that purports to be a form of warrant under section 136 the name of a judge unless that judge signed the warrant; or  (b) states on a form of warrant under that section a matter that, to the inspector’s knowledge, departs in a material particular from the terms of the warrant signed by the judge under that section; or  (c) purports to execute, or presents to another person, a document that purports to be a form of warrant under that section that the inspector knows:  (i) has not been approved by a judge under that section; or  (ii) departs in a material particular from the terms of a warrant signed by a judge under that section; or  (d) gives to a judge a form of warrant under that section that is not the form of warrant that the inspector purported to execute. Penalty: 120 penalty units or imprisonment for 2 years, or both. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  Application of the Australian Consumer Law  Schedule 2  Trade Practices Amendment (Australian Consumer Law) Act (No. 2) 2010 Subdivision G—Miscellaneous"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "040d2b3689dd1d4c9661e938f4633914f2c0aa0a1fbc3bc0047f554c0b16fd20" ;
    cof:pageNumber "318"^^xsd:integer ;
