doc:sch-2-sec-140e-ss-3 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "140E(3) Commonwealth consent to conferral of functions etc. on" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(3) The Commonwealth entity cannot perform a duty or function, or exercise a power, under an application law unless the conferral of the function or power, or the imposition of the duty, is in accordance with an agreement between the Commonwealth and the State or Territory concerned." ;
    cof:pageNumber "343"^^xsd:integer .

concept:act-sch-2-sec-140f a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "140F How duty is imposed"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-140f ;
    values:originalText "Application  (1) This section applies if an application law purports to impose a duty on a Commonwealth entity. Note: Section 140G sets out when such a law imposes a duty on a Commonwealth entity. State or Territory legislative power sufficient to support duty  (2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Act (or any other law of the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:  (a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State or Territory concerned; and  (b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the entity. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  Schedule 2  Application of the Australian Consumer Law" ;
    skos:definition "Application  (1) This section applies if an application law purports to impose a duty on a Commonwealth entity. Note: Section 140G sets out when such a law imposes a duty on a Commonwealth entity. State or Territory legislative power sufficient to support duty  (2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Act (or any other law of the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:  (a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State or Territory concerned; and  (b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the entity. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2012C00868  Schedule 2  Application of the Australian Consumer Law"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "e7ad522ea327e5ba95534ef8355038ef7f7b86328c7cf9175886f60464c40d28" ;
    cof:pageNumber "343"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-140f-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:act-sch-2-sec-140f-ss-2 ;
